ough Aerospace and Industrial segments. The Aerospace segment offers fuel pumps, metering units, actuators, air valves, specialty valves, fuel nozzles, and thrust reverser actuation systems for turbine engines and nacelles, as well as flight deck controls, actuators, servocontrols, motors, and sensors for aircraft. This segment also provides maintenance, repair and overhaul, and other services to commercial airlines, repair facilities, military depots, third party repair shops, and other end users. Its Industrial segment offers actuators, valves, pumps, fuel injection systems, solenoids, ignition systems, control systems, electronics and software, and sensors used on industrial gas turbines, steam turbines, compressors, and reciprocating engines, as well as aftermarket products and related services. The company primarily serves OEMs and equipment packagers. Woodward, Inc. was founded in 1870 and is headquartered in Fort Collins, Colorado.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Charles P. Blankenship Jr. | CEO & Chairman | Age 58 | Compensation: $4.6M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 11K people. Revenue per employee of $389K ranks in the above the sector median among Industrials peers, with revenue growing 7%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $29K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $3K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 12\u00d7 revenue per employee (reasonable relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (1/10).","financial_summary":["Woodward, Inc. is a large-cap industrials business with a $20.5B market cap on $4.4B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +14% over the past three years and +8% annually across the full decade, the kind of sustained demand persistence that marks a compounder.","Returns on capital run at 15% against a 9% cost of capital, a 6-point spread that creates value, though narrow enough that competitive pressure could close it.","The business throws off $327M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 7 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.11\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably conservative at 1.0\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and interest coverage of 15\u00d7 is fortress-like.","Shareholder yield of 3.9% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome, and within its sector the business captures 1.6\u00d7 its fair share of profits, a structural advantage visible in the numbers."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Price sits 127% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left, and the cushion against estimate error has been bid away (confirmed by 2 other valuation measures)","sev":"red","cat":"valuation","magnitude":1.2720487035201427},{"text":"Significant insider selling (net $7.2M)","sev":"amber","cat":"insider","dollars":-7229223.16},{"text":"Cyclicality and capital intensity \u2014 order books turn on a dime, and the heavy fixed cost base that creates the moat also magnifies the downturn.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Supply chain reshoring costs and labour shortages \u2014 onshoring mandates raise capex requirements while a shrinking skilled-labour pool inflates operating costs.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Tariff and trade-policy exposure \u2014 industrial exporters sit squarely in the crosshairs of protectionist policy, and retaliatory tariffs can close markets without warning.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Reinvestment rate supports 10% organic growth capacity \u2014 capital is compounding inside the business","Operating margins 9% above sector median \u2014 the quantitative trace of pricing power","Altman Z comfortably in the safe zone 